        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 2628,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 768,
            "position_y": -10,
            "width": 598,
            "height": 1157,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the middle value of an array: sort the values and take the one in the middle.\n\nHalf the values are below the median and half above. Unlike the mean it does not care how FAR the extreme values are, only which side of the middle they fall - so one glitch, a dropped frame read as 0 or a spike of 1000, hardly moves it. Reach for it when the data has occasional bad values and you want the typical one: a sensor that sometimes jumps, a tracker that sometimes loses lock.\n\nODD AND EVEN COUNTS:\nWith an odd number of values the median is the middle one: 1 2 3 4 100 gives 3. With an even number there are two middle values and the median is halfway between them: 1 2 3 100 gives 2.5. So the median is not always one of the values.\n\nTHE axis MENU:\nWith no axis (the default) the whole array is reduced to one number. With axis 0 the reduction runs DOWN each column, giving one result per column; with axis 1 it runs ALONG each row, giving one result per row. For a table of 5 frames (rows) of 4 sensors (columns), axis 0 answers \"what did each sensor read on average\", axis 1 \"what did all the sensors read on average at each frame\". The axis named is the one that disappears: a 5 by 4 array gives 4 values on axis 0, 5 on axis 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.median <axis: int>\nWith no argument there is no axis: the median of the whole array.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.median\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list or a single number is turned into one). Every array that arrives is sorted (a copy - the input is untouched), its middle taken and sent. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nWhich way to reduce - see THE axis MENU. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next array uses it. The menu offers None (the whole array) and the axes the last array had (None and 0 for a 1-D array); a message None into the axis inlet also returns to the whole array. An axis the array does not have is treated as its last axis for that array only: the choice is kept, so one 1-D array through np.median 1 does not stop the 2-D arrays that follow from being averaged along axis 1.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmedian:\nWith no axis, one number. With an axis, an array one dimension smaller, one median per column (axis 0) or per row (axis 1) - see the axis demo on the np.mean page, which works the same way.\n\nTHE OUTLIER (top demo):\nNine readings: eight close to 5, one glitch of 20. The median is 5.1; the mean is 6.68. Drag the 20 up to 1000 - the median does not move at all, because the glitch is still just \"the one at the top\". Drag it below 5 and the median moves only a step, to the next value in line. Drag two or three values far away and the median finally shifts: it resists outliers only while they are fewer than half.\n\nODD AND EVEN (lower demo):\nClick the two messages. Five values give the middle one, 3; four give the average of the two in the middle, 2.5. In both, the 100 makes no difference to the median; the mean of the same values would be 22 or 26.5.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.mean is the average, pulled about by outliers. np.robust_mean averages the values near the median - smoother than the median, nearly as hard to fool. np.nanmedian skips missing (nan) values. np.sort shows the order the median is read from. t.median is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 590,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1141,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the middle value of an array: sort the values and take the one in the middle.\n\nHalf the values are below the median and half above. Unlike the mean it does not care how FAR the extreme values are, only which side of the middle they fall - so one glitch, a dropped frame read as 0 or a spike of 1000, hardly moves it. Reach for it when the data has occasional bad values and you want the typical one: a sensor that sometimes jumps, a tracker that sometimes loses lock.\n\nODD AND EVEN COUNTS:\nWith an odd number of values the median is the middle one: 1 2 3 4 100 gives 3. With an even number there are two middle values and the median is halfway between them: 1 2 3 100 gives 2.5. So the median is not always one of the values.\n\nTHE axis MENU:\nWith no axis (the default) the whole array is reduced to one number. With axis 0 the reduction runs DOWN each column, giving one result per column; with axis 1 it runs ALONG each row, giving one result per row. For a table of 5 frames (rows) of 4 sensors (columns), axis 0 answers \"what did each sensor read on average\", axis 1 \"what did all the sensors read on average at each frame\". The axis named is the one that disappears: a 5 by 4 array gives 4 values on axis 0, 5 on axis 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.median <axis: int>\nWith no argument there is no axis: the median of the whole array.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.median\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list or a single number is turned into one). Every array that arrives is sorted (a copy - the input is untouched), its middle taken and sent. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nWhich way to reduce - see THE axis MENU. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next array uses it. The menu offers None (the whole array) and the axes the last array had (None and 0 for a 1-D array); a message None into the axis inlet also returns to the whole array. An axis the array does not have is treated as its last axis for that array only: the choice is kept, so one 1-D array through np.median 1 does not stop the 2-D arrays that follow from being averaged along axis 1.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmedian:\nWith no axis, one number. With an axis, an array one dimension smaller, one median per column (axis 0) or per row (axis 1) - see the axis demo on the np.mean page, which works the same way.\n\nTHE OUTLIER (top demo):\nNine readings: eight close to 5, one glitch of 20. The median is 5.1; the mean is 6.68. Drag the 20 up to 1000 - the median does not move at all, because the glitch is still just \"the one at the top\". Drag it below 5 and the median moves only a step, to the next value in line. Drag two or three values far away and the median finally shifts: it resists outliers only while they are fewer than half.\n\nODD AND EVEN (lower demo):\nClick the two messages. Five values give the middle one, 3; four give the average of the two in the middle, 2.5. In both, the 100 makes no difference to the median; the mean of the same values would be 22 or 26.5.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.mean is the average, pulled about by outliers. np.robust_mean averages the values near the median - smoother than the median, nearly as hard to fool. np.nanmedian skips missing (nan) values. np.sort shows the order the median is read from. t.median is the PyTorch version."
        },
